1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Streets are harder than laboratories. A parked van can hide a pedestrian,

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,000
and a robot that simply keeps walking can make the wrong decision.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
This is STREETWISE.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,000
We recreated a street in Chennai, India.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:19,000
At first, the robot just keeps walking.

6
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:22,000
A person steps out from behind the van,

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:27,000
and the robot bumps into them.

8
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,333
Now replay the same encounter with trained navigation. PPO has learned when

9
00:00:32,333 --> 00:00:37,666
to slow down and wait before proceeding. The walking policy itself stays

10
00:00:37,666 --> 00:00:43,000
frozen. Every robot joint you see comes from recorded MuJoCo physics.

11
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,500
These are the original training results from Weights and Biases.

12
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:53,300
Across sixty-four pedestrian tests, successful crossings rose from forty-two to sixty-four.

13
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:58,000
The robot also learned to give people more space.

14
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Training on a molab Blackwell GPU adds lateral movement, moving cars and

15
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:08,000
road defects. Mixed-hazard completion rises from twenty-eight to sixty-four out of sixty-four.

16
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Rain changes ground friction; fog limits tracking range. The first weather test

17
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:18,000
still has one failure.

18
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
A separate world-model lab predicts futures and learns from simulated experience, with

19
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Weave tracing its analyst. For robot training, a challenge ladder introduces harder

20
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,000
conditions after two perfect validation checks. Test results never control advancement.

21
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Compare both policies side by side, or run live MuJoCo with Chennai

22
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
geometry. Marimo, checkpoints, official SDK evidence and startup instructions make the results

23
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,000
inspectable.

24
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:50,000
This is simulation, not a physical robot safety claim. STREETWISE helps teams

25
00:01:50,250 --> 00:01:51,600
Thank you.
